WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.220
AMD GPU systems use a different ecosystem.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:07.600
The AMDGPU kernel driver provides core Linux support for many AMD devices,

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.554
while ROCm supports GPU compute workflows at a high level.

00:00:10.554 --> 00:00:15.081
HIP is a programming model associated with portable GPU compute in the AMD ecosystem.

00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:18.610
Support depends on hardware generation, distribution, kernel version,

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:21.295
user-space components, and application compatibility.

00:00:21.295 --> 00:00:25.260
Vulkan compute paths may be useful for certain local inference or graphics-adjacent

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:26.130
workloads,

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:28.495
especially when a runtime supports Vulkan acceleration.

00:00:28.495 --> 00:00:33.040
Mesa and related user-space graphics components can also matter for integrated GPUs,

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.200
desktop systems, and open driver paths.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.570
These options are not interchangeable with CUDA, and application support varies.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:43.120
Integrated GPUs and discrete GPUs have different expectations.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:46.945
Integrated GPUs may share system memory and be useful for lightweight workloads or display

00:00:46.945 --> 00:00:47.710
tasks.

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:51.220
Discrete GPUs usually provide stronger acceleration and dedicated VRAM,

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:54.550
but they also require power, cooling, and driver compatibility.

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:59.585
Some systems may use CPU fallback when GPU acceleration is not available or not worth the

00:00:59.585 --> 00:01:00.230
complexity.

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:03.596
The practical decision is to match the compute path to the workload and hardware.

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:07.880
ROCm may be appropriate when the GPU and distribution are supported and the AI framework

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:09.350
expects it.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:10.675
Vulkan may be enough for certain local runtimes.

00:01:10.675 --> 00:01:14.385
CPU fallback may be acceptable for small models or low-volume tasks.

00:01:14.385 --> 00:01:17.320
The right path is the one that works reliably for the actual system.
